[%cacheread 0] [%cachewrite 2293] The key question is whether the queen and
bishop are safe. The white king on c2 is not next to a1, and the rook's path
along the second rank is blocked by its own king. So the queen on a1 and the
bishop on h2 are both safe for now. Black is well ahead in material, with
queen, bishop and four pawns against rook, knight and three pawns. The bishop
on h2 risks being hemmed in by g3. I'll bring it to the centre with Be5. From
there it attacks the knight on d4. The e3 pawn defends the knight, but the
bishop also prepares Bxd4 followed by recapturing with the queen, and it puts
pressure on f2. If White plays f4 or Nf3 to chase the bishop, it can simply
retreat. } 52. f4 Bxd4 { [%emt 0:00:24] [%think 1732] [%out 1856] [%in 2]
